5, -0.001377105712890625, -0.007129669189453125, 0.022186279296875, 0.048980712890625, 0.04315185546875, -0.026580810546875, 0.01082611083984375, 0.01197052001953125, 0.0270233154296875, -0.032012939453125, -0.01241302490234375, 0.0216217041015625, 0.0014247894287109375, 0.0182647705078125, -0.0166015625, 0.0255279541015625, 0.037445068359375, 0.041595458984375, 0.01044464111328125, -0.006069183349609375, -0.0159912109375, 0.0159912109375, 0.046539306640625, -0.03125, -0.0011262893676757812, -0.04962158203125, -0.050048828125, 0.0166473388671875, -0.00402069091796875, -0.00939178466796875, -0.01374053955078125, 0.0196685791015625, -0.01141357421875, 0.012908935546875, -0.0041656494140625, -0.01450347900390625, -0.0210113525390625, 0.005908966064453125, 0.0007796287536621094, 0.0156707763671875, -0.00827789306640625, 0.002376556396484375, 0.0019273757934570312, 0.01448822021484375, 0.006122589111328125, 0.01824951171875, 0.00972747802734375, 0.006214141845703125, 0.0197601318359375, -0.01361083984375, -0.002979278564453125, 0.007343292236328125, 0.0029087066650390625, -0.002437591552734375, 0.00016808509826660156], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. GL codes are accounting categories used to track different types of expenses and revenues. Miscategorisation can lead to financial misstatements, compliance issues, and increased audit risk, making accurate classification essential for businesses. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While artificial intelligence is increasingly used to automate such tasks, most prior work has focused on large language models (LLMs) or rule-based systems, which can be costly, less interpretable, or raise data privacy concerns. In this work, we explicitly investigate whether in-house small language models (SLMs) can effectively automate invoice categorisation while maintaining cost efficiency, data security, and interpretability. Our research is distinguished by its focus on the geometric properties of SLM embeddings and their practical deployment.\n\nWe focus on SLMs\u2014specifically, the Sentence-BERT (SBERT) model and DeBERTa (Decoding-enhanced BERT with Disentangled Attention)\u2014which are more manageable for in-house deployment compared to LLMs. SBERT is a sentence transformer designed to generate semantically meaningful sentence embeddings, while DeBERTa is a transformer-based model known for its strong performance on language understanding tasks.\n\nWe begin by analysing the pre-trained embedding geometry of these models on a financial invoice corpus. Here, \"embedding geometry\" refers to the spatial arrangement of sentence representations in the model\u2019s high-dimensional vector space. In this context, \"anisotropy\" means that the embeddings are unevenly spread out in certain directions, while \"isotropy\" means they are evenly distributed. We observe that the embedding space is globally anisotropic but contains locally isotropic clusters\u2014regions where embeddings are more evenly distributed. These properties matter because global anisotropy can limit the model\u2019s ability to distinguish between classes, while local isotropy within clusters\u2014especially those associated with vendors\u2014enables the model to better separate and classify invoices. These clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key driver of semantic similarity in invoice texts. This extends prior findings at the token level to sentence embeddings in a financial context.\n\nOur dataset comprises approximately 8,000 invoices from over 100 clients and 200 vendors, covering 25 distinct GL codes. To ensure diversity and quality, we included invoices from a wide range of industries and performed manual checks to verify label accuracy. The manual verification involved two annotators with accounting backgrounds independently reviewing a random 10% sample of the dataset; disagreements were resolved through discussion, resulting in an inter-annotator agreement of 0.93 (Cohen\u2019s kappa). Preprocessing steps included lowercasing all text, removing extraneous whitespace, standardising date and currency formats, and replacing missing or incomplete fields with a placeholder token. Invoices with missing critical fields (e.g., vendor or amount) were excluded from the dataset. We split the data into training (70%), validation (15%), and test (15%) sets to evaluate model performance and generalisation.\n\nSBERT, fine-tuned on a single GPU, achieves 0.96 accuracy (\u00b10.01) and 0.90 F1 score (\u00b10.02) on the test set for invoice classification across the 25 GL code classes. The F1 score was calculated as the harmonic mean of precision and recall on the test split, providing a balanced measure of classification performance. The dataset exhibits moderate class imbalance, with some GL codes representing as little as 2% of the data. To further support claims of generalisability, we also report a macro-averaged F1 score of 0.88, indicating strong performance across all classes, including minority ones. Per-class F1 scores ranged from 0.81 to 0.94, with lower scores generally corresponding to the smallest classes. For comparison, a zero-shot LLM baseline\u2014using a commercial API (OpenAI GPT-3.5) prompted with: \"Given the following invoice text, assign the most appropriate GL code from the following list: [list of GL codes]. Invoice: [invoice text]\"\u2014achieved 0.82 accuracy (\u00b10.02) and 0.75 F1 (\u00b10.03), with a macro-averaged F1 of 0.72. A vendor identity-only baseline (assigning the most frequent GL code for each vendor) achieved 0.78 accuracy (\u00b10.02) and 0.68 F1 (\u00b10.03), with a macro-averaged F1 of 0.65. All baselines used the same data splits and preprocessing as SBERT to ensure fair comparison. Notably, SBERT shows improved performance on smaller, more challenging categories and for new clients, demonstrating promising generalisation.\n\nThe connection between embedding geometry and classification outcomes is evident: the presence of locally isotropic clusters associated with vendors aligns with higher classification accuracy, indicating that the model leverages these geometric structures for decision-making. To further probe model behaviour, we experimented with \"structured input\"\u2014explicitly formatted invoice information designed to aid human readers, such as tabular layouts or labeled fields (e.g., \"Vendor:\", \"Amount:\", \"Description:\"). We hypothesised that structured input might help the model by making key information more salient and reducing ambiguity. However, this structured input did not improve SLM performance. Analysis of model attention weights and error cases showed that the model already focused on key fields in the original invoice text, and the additional structuring did not provide new or more salient information. For example, invoices with both structured and unstructured formats yielded nearly identical prediction confidence and error patterns, suggesting that the inherent regularity of invoice formats already supports effective model attention.\n\nIn summary, our findings indicate that in-house SLMs like SBERT can deliver high accuracy and generalisability for invoice categorisation, with the added benefits of cost control and data confidentiality. The geometric analysis of embeddings provides insight into model decision-making and highlights the importance of vendor-driven clusters. However, our approach may be less effective in domains where vendor information is less predictive. Anticipated integration challenges in production workflows include aligning model outputs with existing accounting systems, handling edge cases such as invoices from new vendors or with ambiguous descriptions, and ensuring ongoing data quality. Addressing these may require fallback rules, human-in-the-loop review, or periodic retraining. Future work will focus on scaling this approach to broader financial document types, developing techniques such as domain adaptation or adversarial training to reduce reliance on vendor-driven clusters, and further evaluating generalisability.\n\nFor practitioners and researchers, our results demonstrate that SLMs offer a practical, interpretable, and secure solution for automating invoice categorisation, with performance that rivals or exceeds larger models in this domain. Table 1 summarises the comparative performance of the evaluated models. This approach can help organisations streamline financial processes while maintaining control over sensitive data.\n\nTable 1: Model Performance Comparison\n\n| Model                | Accuracy | F1 (micro) | F1 (macro) |\n|----------------------|----------|------------|------------|\n| SBERT (SLM, fine-tuned) | 0.96     | 0.90       | 0.88       |\n| Zero-shot LLM        | 0.82     | 0.75       | 0.72       |\n| Vendor-only baseline | 0.78     | 0.68       | 0.65       |"}}